+            <li>Validate one generated data artifact the way Dan does: define a value that must not appear, search for it, and record the zero-hit proof before trusting ✅.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass evidence</h4><ul><li>State transitions carry real ADW IDs and real commit hashes or honest failure reasons.</li><li>The clock dependency starts only after all earlier tasks in its group reach ✅.</li><li>Model and workflow tags route as predicted.</li><li>One sparse worktree and one application artifact receive independent verification.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 8.3 · Read app4 as a PITER factory</h3>
+          <p><strong>Time:</strong> 15–25 minutes · <strong>Purpose:</strong> understand specialized routing and claim semantics without requiring a Notion account.</p>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
+            <li>Enter <code>tac8_app4__agentic_prototyping</code>. Read the trigger’s claim→route→spawn path and <code>.claude/commands/plan_uv_mcp.md</code>.</li>
+            <li>Run Claude and execute: <code>/plan_uv_mcp test0001 "Create a concise and minimal MCP server that provides system information: memory, cpu, disk"</code>.</li>
+            <li>Open the generated <code>specs/plan-*-uv-mcp.md</code>. Grade it against metadata, description, relevant files, ordered steps, and validation commands.</li>
+            <li>In the source and README, prove that <code>{{prototype: uv_mcp}}</code> routes to <code>/plan_uv_mcp</code>, and <code>continue - &lt;prompt&gt;</code> is the HIL iteration trigger.</li>
+            <li>Locate the immediate <em>In progress</em> + ADW ID claim transition and explain the duplicate-pickup failure it prevents.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass evidence</h4><ul><li>A real Plan-Format-conformant UV MCP plan exists inside the course app.</li><li>PITER maps to the exact board, poller, <code>trees/</code>, and review states.</li><li>The claim happens before detached delegation in your source trace.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 8.4 · Verify app5’s AEA exception</h3>
+          <p><strong>Time:</strong> 15–25 minutes · <strong>Purpose:</strong> prove two concurrent in-product agents preserve focused conversational continuity.</p>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
+            <li>Enter <code>tac8_app5__nlq_to_sql_aea</code>. Copy <code>app/server/.env.sample</code> to <code>app/server/.env</code>, add the required keys, install the tracked client/server dependencies, and update the course’s machine-specific <code>CLAUDE_CODE_PATH</code> in <code>scripts/aea_server_start.sh</code>.</li>
+            <li>Run <code>./scripts/start.sh</code>. In another terminal run <code>./scripts/aea_server_start.sh</code>. Confirm the application at <code>http://localhost:5173</code> and AEA server on port 8743.</li>
+            <li>Press Cmd+P on macOS or Ctrl+P elsewhere, run <code>/aea</code>, and ask, “what is this codebase about?”</li>
+            <li>Create a second AEA window and request one small UI change inside this same course application. Do not edit the application by hand.</li>
+            <li>Refresh the page. Verify both agents reconnect with their histories. Record their <code>agent_id</code> values and find stored <code>cc_session_id</code> values in the server’s persisted state.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass evidence</h4><ul><li>Two concurrent AEA windows: one Q&amp;A, one code change.</li><li>Both reconnect after refresh.</li><li>You can trace <code>agent_id</code> → SQLite row → <code>cc_session_id</code> → <code>--resume</code>.</li><li>The code change came through the AEA workflow, not your editor.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 8.5 · Stretch the course layer safely</h3>
+          <p>Choose one. All work remains inside the TAC-8 course codebase.</p>
+          <ul>
+            <li><strong>New problem class in app1:</strong> create a <code>/bugfix</code> or <code>/docs</code> template meta prompt, wire it to the shared implementer, and prove team standards appear in a generated plan.</li>
+            <li><strong>Blocked-chain proof in app2:</strong> design task one to fail validation and task two as <code>[⏰]</code>; prove the second never starts and the first records an honest reason.</li>
+            <li><strong>Trigger surgery in app2:</strong> change polling and concurrency through supported flags, then add one new workflow tag and ADW route in the course app.</li>
+            <li><strong>Real app4 board:</strong> configure the course app’s Notion MCP and database ID, run one <code>{{prototype: uv_script}}</code> card with <code>--once</code>, and return it to the board’s review flow.</li>
+            <li><strong>AEA restart:</strong> keep the AEA pointed at app5, restart the server, and prove the persisted course-agent conversation reconnects.</li>
+          </ul>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Submission checklist</h3>
+        <ul class="lab-checklist">
+          <li>Zero application code typed by hand.</li>
+          <li>Four gateway output files produced and correctly explained.</li>
+          <li>Composed app1 workflow produced a plan, runnable shell application, and two-phase summary.</li>
+          <li>app2 board shows verifiable commits or honest failures, dependency behavior, tag routing, and sparse isolation.</li>
+          <li>app4 produced a valid specialized plan and a correct PITER/claim trace.</li>
+          <li>app5 ran two resumable agents inside the application.</li>
+          <li>Oral check completed without notes: layers, ≥50% rule, MVAL, gateway receipts, report contract, task states and tags, PITER→PITE prerequisite, focused-agent exception, final question, and three exemptions.</li>
+        </ul>
+
+        <h3>Grading rubric · 24 points</h3>
+        <div class="rubric-grid" role="table" aria-label="TAC-8 lab grading rubric">
+          <div class="rubric-head" role="columnheader">Dimension</div><div class="rubric-head" role="columnheader">0 · Missing</div><div class="rubric-head" role="columnheader">1 · Partial</div><div class="rubric-head" role="columnheader">2 · Mastered</div>
+          <div>Layer discipline</div><div>Hand-edited application code</div><div>Mostly stayed in ring; weak classification</div><div>Zero hand code; every checkpoint classified and defended</div>
+          <div>Gateway observability</div><div>No receipts</div><div>Files exist but roles blur</div><div>All four files explained with identity and consumer</div>
+          <div>Prompt contract</div><div>Cannot trace artifact</div><div>Finds plan but misses coupling</div><div>Traces report→regex→implement and predicts failure</div>
+          <div>Todone state</div><div>Board not run</div><div>Runs without dependency/routing proof</div><div>States, tags, commits, block, and isolation all proved</div>
+          <div>PITER factory</div><div>No mapping</div><div>Names letters generically</div><div>Maps real source artifacts and duplicate-claim prevention</div>
+          <div>AEA continuity</div><div>No working agents</div><div>One agent or no resume proof</div><div>Two agents, refresh/restart continuity, full identity trace</div>
+          <div>Feedback judgment</div><div>Drops review by assertion</div><div>Mentions validation</div><div>Uses KPIs, encoded checks, and streak before PITE</div>
+          <div>Teach-back</div><div>Recites terms only</div><div>Explains parts independently</div><div>Connects allocation→MVAL→composition→orchestration→PITE</div>
+          <div>Safety</div><div>Copies bypass practice broadly</div><div>Notes risk after running</div><div>Reads prompts first, scopes repo, and distinguishes production controls</div>
+          <div>Evidence quality</div><div>Claims completion</div><div>Provides screenshots or hashes alone</div><div>Combines receipts, hashes, source trace, validations, and explanation</div>
+          <div>Problem-class thinking</div><div>One-off request only</div><div>Names a class vaguely</div><div>Builds or critiques a reusable class with a transfer test</div>
+          <div>Final question</div><div>Cannot state it</div><div>States it without exemptions</div><div>Uses it daily and names all three legitimate exemptions</div>
+        </div>
+        <p class="fine">Mastery threshold: 20/24, with no zero in safety, layer discipline, or feedback judgment.</p>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="mastery">
+        <span class="section-number">13</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Cumulative mastery · retrieve the whole ring</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Do this section after the lab and without reopening the chapter. Recognition is not the target. You should be able to reconstruct the architecture, diagnose its failures, and decide where engineering time belongs.</p>
+
+        <h3>Round 1 · Twelve retrieval prompts</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Define the two layers by responsibility and give Dan’s two-ingredient definition of the ring.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The application layer is classical product software; the agentic layer is deterministic ADW code combined with non-deterministic prompts/models to operate it.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What is the MVAL, and what is the single scripting-language rule?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>adws/</code>, <code>.claude/commands/</code>, and <code>specs/</code>; use any language that composes code, references files, and remains isolated from the application layer.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Name the four app1 gateway receipts in order.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>cc_raw_output.jsonl</code>, <code>cc_raw_output.json</code>, <code>cc_final_object.json</code>, and <code>custom_summary_output.json</code>.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Why is a Plan Format more than Markdown formatting?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It compiles engineering standards into a reusable generated artifact, including research, ordered tasks, and validation that a higher-order prompt can execute.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">5. What exact contract couples <code>/chore</code> to <code>adw_chore_implement.py</code>?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The Report section must return the created <code>specs/chore-*.md</code> path so deterministic code can extract and pass it to <code>/implement</code>.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">6. Recite Todone’s states, dependency rule, and two routing dimensions.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Pending → in progress → complete or failed; a clock waits for every earlier task in its worktree to be ✅; tags route model and workflow.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">7. Why is a worktree plus sparse checkout called double isolation?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The task gets a separate Git working tree and that tree exposes only the target app directory.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">8. Map PITER onto app4 and name the claim invariant.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Notion card, poller, <code>trees/</code>, board review; set In progress plus ADW ID before spawning.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">9. State the focus rule, exception, and resume mechanism.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>One agent, one prompt, one purpose; AEA conversation is the exception; SQLite maps <code>agent_id</code> to <code>cc_session_id</code>, passed through <code>--resume</code>.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">10. What makes it safe to remove the R?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Encoded feedback and a proven reliability streak showing human review has become the bottleneck.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">11. Name the four agentic KPIs and directions.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Attempts down, task size up, success streak up, human presence toward zero.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">12. State the final question and all three application-layer exemptions.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Am I working on the agentic layer or in the application layer? Legitimate app-layer reasons: deadline, manual testing, or in-loop verification of prompts intended for out-loop use.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 2 · Diagnose four broken systems</h3>
+        <div class="proof-grid">
+          <article><strong>Broken handoff</strong><p>A planner produces a file but the builder never runs. Diagnose the Report contract, returned filename, regex, file existence, and phase-one success before changing the model.</p></article>
+          <article><strong>Duplicate prototype</strong><p>Two agents start the same Notion card. Inspect whether the In progress + ADW ID claim happened before detached delegation and whether eligibility excludes claimed states.</p></article>
+          <article><strong>Stuck clock</strong><p>A <code>[⏰]</code> task never starts. Read all earlier tasks in that worktree; any non-✅ state, especially ❌, correctly blocks it.</p></article>
+          <article><strong>Forgetful AEA</strong><p>An agent loses history after refresh. Trace active application IDs, SQLite persistence, stored Claude session ID, and the <code>--resume</code> branch.</p></article>
+          <article><strong>Unsafe “ZTE”</strong><p>A team deletes review after two passes. Ask for encoded validations, attempt trend, task-size evidence, success streak, and useful-review rate.</p></article>
+          <article><strong>Thin calendar</strong><p>The team understands every mechanism but spends no time on it. Audit work blocks, name exemptions, and template the most repeated manual problem class.</p></article>
+        </div>
+
+        <h3>Round 3 · Ninety-second teach-back</h3>
+        <div class="teach-back">
+          <p>Without notes, teach this progression:</p>
+          <ol><li>Separate application and agentic responsibilities.</li><li>Start the ring with prompts, plans, and ADWs.</li><li>Make one agent call observable.</li><li>Generate structured plans and hand them across explicit contracts.</li><li>Add triggers, task state, routing, and isolated environments.</li><li>Keep agents focused; resume only when conversation is the purpose.</li><li>Encode feedback, measure reliability, and drop review only when earned.</li><li>Ask the final question every day and allocate at least half your time to the ring.</li></ol>
+          <p class="fine">Mastery sounds causal: each capability solves a failure exposed by the previous layer. A list of folder names is not enough.</p>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="glossary">
+        <span class="section-number">14</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Glossary · the course’s operating language</h2>
+        <div class="glossary-grid">
+          <article><h3>Agentic layer</h3><p>Deterministic ADW code plus non-deterministic agentic prompts and models, wrapped around the application to operate it.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Application layer</h3><p>The classical system: product code, data, database, infrastructure, and DevOps.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>MVAL</h3><p>Minimum Viable Agentic Layer: <code>adws/</code>, <code>.claude/commands/</code>, and <code>specs/</code>.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>ADW</h3><p>AI Developer Workflow: executable composition of deterministic code and agentic work.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Template meta prompt</h3><p>A reusable prompt that generates another prompt artifact—usually a plan—in a strict format.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Higher-order prompt</h3><p>A prompt that consumes another prompt or plan as its argument; <code>/implement</code> is the course example.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Plan Format</h3><p>The required shape of a generated plan, including metadata, relevant files, ordered tasks, and validation commands.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Report contract</h3><p>A machine-readable output promise joining a non-deterministic agent phase to deterministic orchestration.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Gateway script</h3><p>The smallest ADW: one agent call plus run identity, environment, retries, and observable outputs.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Todone</h3><p>app2’s to-do→to-DONE multi-agent task system driven by a two-way Markdown task list.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Trigger</h3><p>The mechanism that fires an ADW without a person dispatching it: poller, schedule, webhook, CI event, or product server.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Double isolation</h3><p>A dedicated Git worktree plus sparse checkout containing only the target application directory.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>PITER / PITE</h3><p>Prompt input, Trigger, Environment, Review; drop Review only after feedback loops and reliability evidence make it redundant.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>AEA</h3><p>Agent Embedded Application: an agent interface living inside the product, with durable session continuity where required.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Problem class</h3><p>A recurring family of work captured by a reusable template or workflow, rather than one task instance.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Agentic KPIs</h3><p>Attempts ↓, task size ↑, success streak ↑, and human presence → 0.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Prioritize Agentics</h3><p>Tactic #8: allocate at least half of engineering time to improving the agentic layer as a starting point.</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Final question</h3><p>“Am I working on the agentic layer or in the application layer?”—the daily compression of the course.</p></article>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="question-card">
+          <p>Build the system that builds the system.</p>
+          <small>Then improve the ring until your best engineering practice is executable.</small>
+        </div>
+
+        <nav class="chapter-pagination" aria-label="Chapter pagination">
+          <a href="07-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering.html">← Chapter 07 · ZTE Secret of Agentic Engineering</a>
+          <a href="../index.html">Return to the book hub →</a>
+        </nav>
+      </section>
+
+      <footer class="chapter-footer">
+        <p>Volume I · Tactical Agentic Coding · Chapter 08</p>
+        <p>Source-bound to the TAC-8 extract and tracked course codebase.</p>
+      </footer>
+    </main>
+  </div>
+
+  <script>
+    (function () {
+      var topBar = document.getElementById('top-progress');
+      var railBar = document.getElementById('rail-progress');
+      var label = document.getElementById('progress-label');
+      var sections = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.chapter-section[id]'));
+      var links = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.chapter-nav a'));
+      var progressFramePending = false;
+
+      function updateProgress() {
+        var scrollable = document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight;
+        var ratio = scrollable > 0 ? Math.min(1, Math.max(0, window.scrollY / scrollable)) : 0;
+        var value = Math.round(ratio * 100);
+        if (topBar) topBar.style.width = value + '%';
+        if (railBar) railBar.style.width = value + '%';
+        if (label) label.textContent = value + '%';
+        progressFramePending = false;
+      }
+
+      function requestProgressUpdate() {
+        if (progressFramePending) return;
+        progressFramePending = true;
+        window.requestAnimationFrame(updateProgress);
+      }
+
+      if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
+        var observer = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
+          var visible = entries.filter(function (entry) { return entry.isIntersecting; }).sort(function (a, b) { return b.intersectionRatio - a.intersectionRatio; })[0];
+          if (!visible) return;
+          links.forEach(function (link) {
+            var active = link.getAttribute('href') === '#' + visible.target.id;
+            if (active) link.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location');
+            else link.removeAttribute('aria-current');
+          });
+        }, { rootMargin: '-20% 0px -65% 0px', threshold: [0, .1, .5] });
+        sections.forEach(function (section) { observer.observe(section); });
+      }
+
+      updateProgress();
+      window.addEventListener('scroll', requestProgressUpdate, { passive: true });
+      window.addEventListener('resize', requestProgressUpdate);
+    })();
+  </script>
+    </main>
+  </div>
+</body>
+</html>

